  "text": "In nineteen seventy-six, Peter Chen proposed the entity-relationship model: say what the data means before deciding how to store it.\n\nAn entity-relationship diagram has three pieces. Boxes are entities, the facts inside are attributes, and lines are relationships. PK marks a primary key, and FK a foreign key. Now read the line. One customer places zero or many orders, and each order belongs to exactly one customer. But look how much that one line said. Entities never live alone... the lines between them carry as much meaning as the boxes.",
